  "query_preview": "..\nWednesday through Thursday: An upper level low is making its way \ninland off the PNW coast. A surface low is also ushering in a cold \nfront. Lift and instability associated with both the upper level and \nsurface lows has resulted in isolated convection moving through the \nforecast area. DCAPE values of 1000 J/kg combined with a very dry \nboundary layer will result in main hazards being gusty outflow winds \nup to 40 mph and dry lightning. As the low moves further inland, it \nwill track northeastward through Washington and Idaho. Widespread \nrain showers will move through the area later today, bringing \nbeneficial rains. Much of the forecast area is expected to see 0.10 \ninches of rain or more through early Thursday morning. Areas that \nwill see the highest rainfall amounts will be the mountains, which \ncould see up to an inch of rain. \n\nAs the low passes through, the pressure gradient across the Cascades \nwill increase, bringing strong westerly winds across most of the \nforecast area. Winds will increase late Wednesday morning and \ncontinue through Thursday morning, though the peak of the winds will \nhappen late Wednesday morning through afternoon. NBM shows maximum \nwind gusts 40-45 mph for many places, and up to 55 mph in the Blue \nMountains. With these magnitudes, a Wind Advisory has been issued \nfrom early this morning through 11pm tonight from the Wenatchee and \nWaterville Plateau area eastward, through the Idaho Panhandle. A \nHigh Wind Warning has been issued for the Blue Mountains. Impacts \nfrom these winds are blowing unsecured objects, tree limbs, the \npossibility for a few power outages, and difficult driving \nconditions, especially for high profile vehicles. \n\nFriday through Sunday: A broad upper trough will move through the \narea Saturday, bringing further chances for precipitation. This \nsystem will arrive with anomalously cold 850mb temperatures, nearly \n4-7 degrees colder than this time of year. As the low tracks through \nthe area, it will bring showers, especially to the northern Idaho \nPanhandle and the Cascades. The 25th-75th percentile for snow along \nthe Cascades is 1-4 inches. The basin and lowlands will mostly be \ndry. However, these colder conditions bring the potential for frost \nissues, particularly Saturday evening into Sunday morning, where \nthere is a 40% chance and above for many areas to see low \ntemperatures less than 36 degrees. /AS",
